2.34.1

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (!pskb_may_pull(skb, iph->ihl * 4 + 8)) { ... return; }
raw_icmp_error(skb, protocol, info);
ipprot = rcu_dereference(inet_protos[protocol]);
if (ipprot && ipprot->err_handler) ipprot->err_handler(skb, info);

Reason:
The icmp_socket_deliver function did not check whether the inner protocol equals IPPROTO_RAW (255) before invoking raw socket error handlers. According to man 7 raw, sockets created with IPPROTO_RAW are only for sending, not receiving. Consequently, a malicious ICMP error message encapsulating an inner packet with protocol 255 would be delivered to such a socket, triggering unintended FNHE cache updates and other side effects.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (protocol == IPPROTO_RAW)
	goto out;
out:
	__ICMP_INC_STATS(dev_net_rcu(skb->dev), ICMP_MIB_INERRORS);

Reason:
The fix explicitly checks if the inner protocol is IPPROTO_RAW. If so, it jumps to the out label, which increments the ICMP error statistics and returns without calling raw_icmp_error or any protocol error handler. This ensures that IPPROTO_RAW sockets never receive incoming packets, aligning with the documented behavior and closing the vulnerability.

